    summary:
      'Johnson posted the feed’s first quote-post of an AI assistant’s advice (4,912 likes, 160 reposts, 53 replies at re-read; 3,967 likes, 127 reposts, 43 replies at the 01:13 UTC raw-batch-capture read; November 25, 20:08 UTC): “Same math applies to sleep. / People mistakenly believe it’s wasted time. / 6hr vs 8hr pushes your system near 100% capacity, multiplying errors, brain fog, and biological debt by 12x.” The quote is @Voxyz_ai sharing Gemini 4 Neon’s queuing-theory answer (6,483 likes on the quoted post) — “Deliberately run your time, energy, and resources below 80% capacity… congestion and delay do not scale linearly with how busy you are… at 80% utilization, your delay multiplier is 4. Push yourself to 90%, and it jumps to 9. Squeeze into 98%, and it explodes to 49. Packing that last 18% of ‘extra output’ into your week doesn’t make you 18% more productive—it multiplies your wait times, error rates, and recovery cost by more than twelvefold.” — transcribed from the attached screenshot by OCR at curation time. Johnson’s “12x” and “near 100% capacity” borrow the AI’s delay-curve figures directly, and the sleep mapping has a real laboratory anchor: Van Dongen, Maislin, Mullington, Dinges, “The Cumulative Cost of Additional Wakefulness: Dose-Response Effects on Neurobehavioral Functions and Sleep Physiology From Chronic Sleep Restriction and Total Sleep Deprivation,” Sleep 2003;26(2):117–126 (10.1093/sleep/26.2.117; 48 healthy adults aged 21–38, randomized to 4, 6, or 8 h time in bed for 14 consecutive days) found chronic 6-hour nights produced cumulative, dose-dependent deficits in cognitive performance equivalent to up to 2 nights of total sleep deprivation, with subjects “largely unaware of these increasing cognitive deficits” — exactly the errors-multiply-without-noticing shape the post asserts — and lapses in behavioral alertness near-linearly related to cumulative wakefulness in excess of 15.84 h (implying ~8.16 h daily sleep need to prevent deficit build-up). Boundaries: queuing theory is a metaphor, not a sleep model — the 4/9/49 delay multipliers describe M/M/1-style utilization curves, and no sleep study produces a “12x” multiplier; Van Dongen is 14-day restriction in 48 young adults on psychomotor vigilance and cognitive tasks, not “biological debt,” and acute single-night effects differ from chronic restriction; the subjects were 21–38, not Johnson’s 49; “6hr vs 8hr” sits at or below Johnson’s own tracked October guidance floor of 6.5–7.5 h ideal sleep; the AI’s advice is itself an unreviewed chatbot answer relayed by a third account, and Gemini’s “12x” is its own rhetorical multiplier, not a citation. The dashboard records an attributed AI-advice relay with a verified sleep-restriction anchor — not evidence that sleep scales like a queue, a reader sleep duration, or medical advice.',
