      'Johnson posted the feed’s first warm-up post (1,736 likes, 55 reposts, 117 replies at re-read; 1,541 likes, 46 reposts, 111 replies at the 01:10 UTC raw-capture read; November 24, 17:49 UTC, with a video that was not transcribed): “I warm up for 20 min before any hard workout / > lowers musculoskeletal injury by 30-38% / > reduces severe injuries by up to 50% / > boosts acute power output by 2% to 5% / Passive static stretching alone does not prevent injuries.” The post names no source, but its numbers trace to the structured warm-up literature. The injury figures map onto the neuromuscular-programme trials: Soligard, Myklebust, Steffen, Holme, Silvers, Bizzini, Junge, Dvorak, Bahr, Andersen, “A comprehensive warm-up programme to prevent injuries in young female footballers: cluster randomised controlled trial,” BMJ 2008;337:a2469 (10.1136/bmj.a2469; 1,892 players aged 13–17 across 105 clubs) found the 20-minute FIFA 11+ programme at overall-injury rate ratio 0.68 (a ~32% reduction), acute-injury reductions of 26–38% across outcome types, and severe-injury rate ratio 0.55 — the paper’s own discussion stating “the risk of injury can be reduced by about one third and severe injuries by as much as one half,” the post’s “up to 50%” almost verbatim; a JAMA Pediatrics cluster-randomized trial of a 20-minute coach-led neuromuscular warm-up in female high-school soccer and basketball players (1,492 completing athletes) found gradual-onset lower-extremity injuries down 65% and acute noncontact injuries down 56%. The “2% to 5%” figure matches the warm-up mechanisms literature — the Sports Medicine review “Warm-Up Strategies for Sport and Exercise: Mechanisms and Applications” reports subsequent exercise performance enhanced by 2–5% per 1°C rise in muscle temperature, with ballistic pre-loading activities eliciting 2–5% power increases. The stretching line verifies as the field’s most replicated null: Thacker, Gilchrist, Stroup, Kimsey, “The impact of stretching on sports injury risk: a systematic review of the literature,” Medicine & Science in Sports & Exercise 2004;36(3):371-378 (CDC; six stretching comparisons pooled at OR 0.93, 95% CI 0.78–1.11 — no significant injury reduction), and the randomized trial by Pope, Herbert, Kirwan, and Graham, MSSE 2000 (1,538 army recruits; all-injury HR 0.95, 95% CI 0.77–1.18). Boundaries: the injury evidence is structured neuromuscular programmes — running, strength, balance, and plyometric progressions — tested in adolescent team-sport athletes, not a generic 20-minute warm-up in 49-year-olds; a 2025 Journal of Science and Medicine in Sport viewpoint argues existing designs cannot separate acute warm-up effects from the chronic fitness effects of the programmes implemented during warm-up — the same ambiguity the 2006 JSAMS systematic review (“Does warming up prevent injury in sport?”) flagged when it found “insufficient evidence to endorse or discontinue routine warm-up prior to physical activity to prevent injury”; the 2–5% figure is performance-mechanism literature, not an injury-trial outcome; “up to 50%” is a ceiling, not an average; and the post’s own warm-up lives in an untranscribed video, so the practice behind the numbers cannot be checked. The dashboard records an attributed research claim with a verified programme literature — not a reader warm-up prescription, evidence that any specific 20-minute warm-up prevents injury in any individual, or medical advice.',
