    summary:
      'Johnson posted a psychedelics-and-aging research relay: “LSD as a longevity therapy? / LSD pushes brain gene activity in the opposite direction of aging / In rodents, the effect was stronger than exercise / LSD activated gene networks involved in neuroplasticity and neuronal communication, and reduced amyloid-β damage” (1,250 likes, 116 reposts, 127 replies; November 11, 19:01 UTC, with a study-figure image). The post names no study, but every line traces to one paper published the same morning — Savino, Liaci, Bertani, … Iorio, “Lysergic acid diethylamide reverses aging- and neurodegeneration-associated brain transcriptional programs,” Aging Cell, November 11, 2026 (10.1111/acel.70793; preprint 10.64898/2026.05.26.727809, Human Technopole Milan). Claim-by-claim: “opposite direction of aging” verifies — chronic LSD treatment in the rat medial prefrontal cortex produced gene-expression changes significantly anti-correlated with age-ranked genes across integrated human prefrontal-cortex datasets (3,086 samples spanning ages 2 days to 106 years; Fisher-aggregated p < 2.2 × 10⁻³⁰⁸ for upregulated genes), reproducible across datasets and species. “Stronger than exercise” is true but narrower than it reads: on the paper’s age-reversal p-value ratio, chronic LSD scored highest (average apr = 198) against exercise and enriched-environment positive controls (largest average apr = 136) — a property of transcriptomic signatures, not of brains, bodies, or longevity outcomes. “Activated gene networks involved in neuroplasticity and neuronal communication” verifies — synapse- and dendrite-related GO categories, typically downregulated with aging, were enriched among LSD-upregulated genes. “Reduced amyloid-β damage” verifies in vitro — in primary cortical neurons exposed to 100 nM amyloid-β, subsequent LSD restored MAP2-positive dendritic arborization that amyloid-β had damaged, without affecting architecture on its own. Boundaries: the entire evidence base is preclinical — rats, mice, and cultured neurons; the paper’s own evidence record shows zero human LSD results; the authors name the bulk-tissue limitation themselves (postmortem bulk tissue mixes cell types whose proportions change with age, so single-cell data will be needed); signature reversal is a computational drug-repurposing rationale, not demonstrated slowing of brain aging or cognitive decline; the post names no study; and LSD remains a Schedule I controlled substance in the U.S. outside regulated research and approved access paths. The dashboard records an attributed research relay with a verified same-day source — not evidence that LSD is a longevity therapy, a recommendation, or medical advice.',
