    title: '“My blood glucose is lower than 99.8% of humans”: the feed’s first glucose-percentile claim — the HbA1c/mean-glucose pair is internally consistent under the standard translation (4.6% ↔ eAG 85 vs a claimed 79 mg/dL), and the rust metaphor matches real glycation pathology, but the mortality curve’s own “basement” sits at ~5.5%, 0.9 points above him, and “99.8% of humans” is measured against the world’s diabetic population, not healthy peers',
    date: '2026-11-10',
    badge: 'Measurement',
    kind: 'tweet',
    topic: 'Blood glucose / HbA1c percentile / glycation / metabolic health',
    summary:
      'Johnson posted the feed’s first glucose-percentile claim: “My blood glucose is lower than 99.8% of humans / best month I’ve had: / > mean 79 mg/dL / > best week 77 mg/dL / > HbA1c 4.6% / Elevated blood glucose rusts from within.” (424 likes, 12 reposts, 80 replies; November 10, 19:16 UTC, with a photo). The internal arithmetic is consistent under the standard translation: the ADAG study (Nathan et al., Diabetes Care 2008; 507 subjects; eAG = 28.7 × A1C − 46.7) puts HbA1c 4.6% at an estimated average glucose of 85.3 mg/dL — within the regression’s ±15.7 mg/dL prediction error of the claimed 79 mg/dL CGM month mean, which back-translates to ~4.4% A1c. But the translation at this end is an extrapolation: Labcorp’s reporting guidance calculates eAG only for HbA1c 5.0–15.0% because “estimations in the nondiabetic range have not been verified,” and a CGM interstitial average is not venous plasma. “Elevated blood glucose rusts from within” works as a glycation metaphor: elevated glucose accelerates nonenzymatic glycation and advanced glycation end-product (AGE) formation, and the AGE–RAGE axis generates reactive oxygen species and inflammatory cascades central to diabetic complications (Nature Reviews Endocrinology 2008, ncpendmet0786) — pathology demonstrated at diabetic-range glucose, not a linear dose–response into the low-normal range. The mortality direction verifies with the stop the post doesn’t mention: EPIC-Norfolk found HbA1c continuously associated with cardiovascular disease and mortality across the nondiabetic distribution, lowest rates below 5% (Khaw et al., BMJ 2001; Ann Intern Med 2004, 10.7326/0003-4819-141-6-200409210-00006), but the cohort’s own 11.2-year reanalysis of 17,196 nondiabetic adults (Diabetologia 2011, 10.1007/s00125-011-2162-0) found the all-cause-mortality hazard “virtually constant in the low range,” rising only above approximately 5.5% — a “basement” below which no mortality benefit of further lowering was demonstrated, and his 4.6% sits 0.9 points below it. Boundaries: “99.8% of humans” is against an unnamed cohort that includes the world’s diabetic and prediabetic population, not healthy peers — the same unpublished-percentile pattern as the Prenuvo and Kernel readouts; the three numbers are summary figures with no device, dataset, or method published; and clinically low HbA1c can also reflect shortened red-cell lifespan (hemolysis, blood loss, anemia) rather than superior metabolic health. The dashboard records an attributed N=1 metabolic readout with verified eAG arithmetic and epidemiologic context — not evidence that pushing HbA1c below 5% extends life, a reader glucose target, or medical advice.',
