    summary:
      'Johnson posted the feed’s most detailed microplastics relay yet, quote-posting his own May 7, 2026 “I HAVE NO MICROPLASTICS IN MY BALLS” post: “Up to 100% of men have microplastics in their semen / I got mine to zero. The first documented case. / A 2024 study detected microplastics in 100% of human and dog testicular tissue. / In another study, microplastics were detected in the semen and urine of all of 113 tested men from China. / Researchers broke down plastic type and how they affected sperm quality. / The most common came from packaging (polystyrene, polypropylene, and polyethylene). / PTFE, the synthetic polymer used in non-stick Teflon pans, proved the most lethal, creating 11% lower sperm concentration and 9% fewer total sperm. / Ten studies have measured microplastics in human semen. Five found 100% detection. / The rest from 84% down to 37%, but older tests used standard infrared microscopes that are blind to particles smaller than 10–20 microns. Newer studies used Raman spectroscopy, which can spot particles down to 1 micron. / So in short, 100% prevalence is plausible if not likely.” (1,428 likes, 65 reposts, 104 replies; October 27, 17:37 UTC). Both named studies verify precisely. First: Hu, Garcia, Nihart, Campen et al., “Microplastic presence in dog and human testis…,” Toxicological Sciences, August 2024 (10.1093/toxsci/kfae060) — pyrolysis-GC/MS found microplastics in all 47 canine and all 23 human testes (human mean 328.44 µg/g, PE dominant), exactly “100% of human and dog testicular tissue,” with the authors’ own caveats that postmortem samples “typically did not experience a natural death,” the method is still being refined, and associations “do not imply causality.” Second: “Association of mixed exposure to microplastics with sperm dysfunction: a multi-site study in China,” eBioMedicine, 2024 (10.1016/j.ebiom.2024.105369) — Raman microscopy on 113 men recruited at three reproductive-medicine centers found microplastics in all semen and urine samples (typically 3–5 polymer types; PS/PP/PE most detected, matching “most common came from packaging”), and PTFE-exposed participants showed lower sperm concentration (52.13 vs 58.32 million/mL, p=0.041 — the post’s “11% lower” is 10.6% rounded) and total sperm count (188.90 vs 207.67 million — the post’s “9% fewer”), with logistic-regression OR 4.32 for reduced concentration; the authors’ stated limits: cross-sectional, association not causation, no exposure concentrations, and a fertility-clinic population. The “ten studies / five at 100%” tally is consistent with the published semen-MP literature (100% cohorts: the 36-man Jinan general-population Raman study, Sci Total Environ 2024; the 113-man eBioMedicine study; a 115-man Malaysian Raman cohort detected MPs in all samples) and with detection-frequency reviews noting older FTIR/LD-IR work at lower rates (Zhao et al. 2023: 11/25; MDPI Toxics 2025: 34/45 = 75.6%; Tagliabue et al. 10.1016/j.scitotenv.2023.165922: 6/10) — though Johnson’s exact “84% down to 37%” endpoints match no single published tally we could identify, and his “Raman spots particles down to 1 micron” compresses that Raman resolves sub-micron in principle while real-world bio-matrix protocols vary. The boundaries the relay drops: his own zero result is a self-reported specialty-lab test with no published method, no third-party replication, and no pre-registration — in his own reply he concedes “This test is not widely available, sadly. I had a specialty lab create the test for me and conduct the analysis. It would be great if someone made a test widely available” (60 likes; 17:56 UTC) — so “first documented case” means first self-reported case; the underlying studies are cross-sectional associations in fertility-clinic or postmortem populations, not dose-response evidence that Teflon pans lower sperm counts in readers; and microplastics methodology reviews (Arch Toxicol 2026, 10.1007/s00204-026-04392-1) flag unstandardized nomenclature, small samples, and contamination risk across the whole field, which cuts at “zero” and “100%” claims alike. The dashboard records an attributed research relay with two verified studies and an unreplicated N=1 centerpiece—not evidence that non-stick pans harm readers’ fertility, a validated zero-microplastics protocol, a reader testing recommendation, or medical advice.',
