    summary:
      'Johnson posted his twice-yearly MRI results with an attached report image: “my twice yearly mri results / > subcutaneous and visceral fat - 20s equivalent / > heart 100% score - 20s equivalent / > hippocampal occupancy - 99th percentile / radiologist report: / > my body is indistinguishable from an elite athlete / healthy bodies... / > make more money / > have more sex / > are more fun” (303 likes, 7 reposts, 50 replies; October 27, 21:55 UTC), following up a minute later that “mri scan was done with @prenuvo” (14 likes). The scanner and cadence are consistent with his established disclosure pattern: Prenuvo is the direct-to-consumer whole-body MRI company whose scans he has attributed his imaging to since his public protocol records, and “twice yearly” matches the twice-a-year cadence he has referenced since mid-2025. But the numbers are his summary of a private report he has not published: “20s equivalent” fat and heart scores, a “100%” heart score, and “99th percentile” hippocampal occupancy name no reference cohort, no normative dataset, and no scoring method — the same undisclosed-cohort pattern the dashboard has recorded on every percentile stack (“I am 18,” the Kernel brain-scan percentiles, the October 22 “you look your age” comeback). The line “radiologist report: my body is indistinguishable from an elite athlete” upgrades a quoted verbal impression to the status of a document, and the “healthy bodies make more money / have more sex / are more fun” coda is the same status pitch the feed attached to the October 22 percentile post. The context the post omits: no professional body recommends whole-body MRI screening of asymptomatic people — the American College of Radiology “does not believe there is sufficient evidence to justify recommending total body screening for patients with no clinical symptoms, risk factors or a family history” (ACR statement, April 17, 2023), RANZCR does not recommend it (position statement, July 2025), and the largest systematic review (J Magn Reson Imaging 2019, 10.1002/jmri.26736; 12 studies, 5,373 asymptomatic adults) found a pooled 32.1% prevalence of critical-or-indeterminate incidental findings with a substantial false-positive proportion (16.0% pooled) and no long-term verification of negative scans — the incidentaloma-and-overdiagnosis trade a screening-adjacent post never mentions. The boundaries: all figures are self-reported impressions of an unpublished report; “indistinguishable from an elite athlete” is an attributed quote, not a clinical finding; the scan was paid consumer screening, not a diagnostic workup; and nothing here is evidence that twice-yearly whole-body MRI improves health outcomes for anyone. The dashboard records an attributed measurement disclosure with verified screening-policy context—not a reader imaging recommendation, evidence of youthful equivalence, or medical advice.',
