    topic: 'Bone mechanosensing / PIEZO1 / mouse lifespan claim',
    summary:
      'Johnson posted the feed’s first bone-mechanosensing longevity claim: “Mechanical pressure on shin bones extended mouse lifespan by 80 days (+9.4%). That’s the human equivalent of 7–9 yrs. Bone pressure triggers PIEZO1 sensors to broadcast anti-aging signals to the brain, liver, and immune system. If this translates to humans, my dunk training is flooding my entire body with anti-aging juice.” (2,538 likes, 156 replies, 141 reposts at re-curation; October 24, 01:28 UTC, with an attached paper screenshot). At first review the headline figure traced to no identified paper: the screenshot was Zhao et al., Aging Cell 2026 (10.1111/acel.70687), a Piezo1-agonist bone-mass study with no lifespan endpoint, and the nearest identical result — median lifespan 862→942 days, ~9%, females only — was Selman et al., Science 2009 (10.1126/science.1177221) on S6K1 knockout, with no mechanical loading. Twelve and a half hours later, Johnson replied to his own post: “Here is the correct paper for the stats in the original post. The paper above is also pro-dunk training. Mechanical pressure triggers PIEZO1 to release anabolic prostaglandin E2, which promotes bone formation and reduces resorption.” (50 likes, 6 replies, 2 reposts, 12,489 views; October 24, 13:53 UTC), attaching a screenshot OCR-identified as Bai et al., “Dynamic force sensing by bone delays aging and extends lifespan,” Cell Metabolism, published online October 23, 2026 — one day before the post (10.1016/j.cmet.2026.09.020). The figure now verifies: dynamic tibial axial loading (2 N at 2 Hz, 300 cycles, twice weekly) in mice at 20–24 months of age extended median lifespan by 9.4%/79.5 days (mean +11.9% females, +7.7% males), reversed bone-marrow immunosenescence, reduced liver steatosis, and improved cognition and endurance, with osteocyte-Piezo1 deletion accelerating whole-body aging and PEDF identified as a mechano-responsive anti-aging osteokine — covering the brain, liver, and immune triad the post named. The mechanism foundation verified last pass stands: osteocyte PIEZO1 bone anabolism (Li et al., eLife 2019, 10.7554/eLife.49631), a bone–brain axis (Cai et al., Nature Neuroscience, 10.1038/s41593-026-02422-w), and a mechano-bone-immune axis (Zhang et al., bioRxiv). The correction’s PGE2 sentence also traces, through the same research group’s mechanism work: loading-induced prostaglandin E2 release via Cx43 hemichannels promotes bone formation (Zhao et al., eLife 2022, 10.7554/eLife.74365) — a compressed but directionally accurate chain downstream of PIEZO1. The Selman match stands as coincidence, not source. Boundaries: one mouse study published one day before the post; the loader is an external calibrated device compressing an anesthetized tibia (2 N, 2 Hz, 300 cycles), not jumping, so dunk-training equivalence remains Johnson’s interpretation; the “human equivalent of 7–9 yrs” remains arithmetic on the mouse percentage; and no human loading evidence exists. The dashboard records an attributed research claim, an unusually fast self-correction, and a now-traced figure—not evidence that jumping or dunk training extends life, a reader exercise prescription, or medical advice.',
