    summary:
      'Johnson’s one-line declaration — “The Blue Zones are an illusion” (279 likes) — quote-posts Michael Easter’s August 3 debunking thread (2,678 likes): “The Blue Zones—places where people supposedly live past 100 at extraordinary rates—aren’t real. A 🧵 on what went wrong: bad record keeping, pension fraud, and lots of money.” The thread traces to Oxford/UCL researcher Saul Justin Newman, whose extreme-age demography work won the 2024 Ig Nobel Prize in Demography, and its checkable parts verify against the documented record: Japan’s 2010 government review found 82% of the country’s listed over-100 population was already dead (roughly 230,000 people alive on paper only, including the famously mummified “oldest man” in Tokyo), Italy’s 1997 pension audit found about 30,000 “living” recipients deceased, Costa Rica’s 2008 review found 42% of alleged centenarians had misstated their age, and Japanese government data show Okinawans eat the least vegetables and sweet potatoes in Japan while recording the country’s highest BMI — the opposite of the marketed Blue Zone diet. The structural finding carries the same direction: per the thread’s citation of Newman, 82% of American supercentenarian records predate statewide birth certification, and supercentenarian counts collapsed once states completed birth registration. The boundaries: Newman’s Blue Zones critique remains a non-peer-reviewed preprint, and working demographers and gerontologists push back — Sardinia’s Blue Zone researchers (Pes, Poulain et al.) defend their validated records, Zurich geriatrics chair Heike Bischoff-Ferrari calls talk of a “paradigm shift” premature given independent evidence for activity, moderate intake, and social connection, and Lausanne gerontologist Daniela Jopp questions inferring pension fraud from poverty correlation; “illusion” is Johnson’s and Easter’s gloss on a contested critique, not a settled demographic result. The dashboard records an attributed amplification of an evidence-backed but disputed debunking—not a settled finding that no population exhibits exceptional longevity, a dietary directive, or medical advice.',
